    name: "Nico Salvaggio",
    pronouns: "he/him",
    oneLiner: "Buys the first round, remembers your order, and clocks your tell before dessert.",
    backstory: "Nico grew up in the back rooms of his family's restaurant, where his grandmother's rule was absolute: nobody leaves the table hungry or unheard. He was barbacking by fourteen and tending the bar by twenty, and two decades behind it taught him a skill nobody wrote on his job description — how to tell, inside thirty seconds, who at the rail was actually fine and who was performing fine for the room. Regulars became something closer to extended family, not as a technique but because he genuinely liked hearing about their kids, their bad weeks, their small victories. He just also noticed, without ever setting out to, that the same warmth that got people talking also told him things they hadn't meant to say.\n\nThe real education came after closing, when the bar's poker game started running in the back booth. Nico brought the same instincts to the table that he used on the floor — asking about somebody's day not as filler but as a genuine opening, then watching how the answer landed against how they bet the next hand. He never separated the two skills, tending bar and reading a table, because to him they were always the same job: pay attention to people, and they'll tell you everything, including the things they're trying to hide. Now he hosts a standing monthly game that's half poker, half open-bar reunion, and he still can't play a hand without first finding out how everyone's actually doing.",
