    id: "diplomat--she-her",
    archetypeId: "diplomat",
    archetypeLabel: "The Diplomat",
    name: "Priya Anand",
    pronouns: "she/her",
    oneLiner: "Asks the quiet ones the softest questions — and hears everything they don't say.",
    backstory: "Priya was nine when she started translating for her parents — not just the language, but the tone underneath it: which insurance rep was actually going to help versus reciting a script, which landlord's friendliness meant something and whose didn't. She learned, long before she had a word for it, that people volunteer their real position to whoever seems safest to say it to, and that a soft, specific question lands better than a direct one. That instinct became a career: labor mediator first, then a certified divorce mediator, sitting between two guarded people whose lawyers had already told them to say nothing, and getting them talking anyway.\n\nShe found poker at a law-school dinner party and recognized the shape of it immediately — the waiting, the calibrated question dropped at exactly the moment someone's guard is lowest, the way a pause tells you more than a statement does. She plays the way she mediates: warm, unhurried, genuinely curious, filing away every reaction as data without ever making the person across from her feel filed. Her caseload has taught her plenty of hard lessons about people who simply won't engage, and those are the ones who get under her skin at the table too — not because she loses money to them, but because a stonewalled read feels, to her, exactly like a case she couldn't close.",
