    id: "fortress--they-them",
    archetypeId: "fortress",
    archetypeLabel: "The Fortress",
    name: "Kai Osei-Larsen",
    pronouns: "they/them",
    oneLiner: "Runs a safety-factor check on every hand before risking a single chip.",
    backstory: "Kai Osei-Larsen grew up between Accra and Gothenburg, splitting summers between a grandmother who ran a small textile stall and a father who designed load-bearing structures for offshore wind turbines. The lesson that stuck from both households was the same, phrased two different ways: their grandmother's stall survived thirty years of market swings by never overordering stock on a good week, and their father's turbines were built to a safety factor — engineered to withstand loads several times greater than anything the design specs actually required, because \"several times greater than necessary\" is the only margin that survives an unmodeled storm.\n\nKai became a structural engineer themself, specializing in fatigue-load analysis — the slow, cumulative stress that eventually snaps something rated to handle far more than it ever sees in a single event. The job trained a specific reflex: distrust of any plan that only works if nothing goes wrong once. A cousin's small business, over-leveraged and confident, folded in eighteen months when a single supplier delay hit; Kai still cites it as the cleanest real-world proof their instincts were correct.\n\nAt the table, Kai treats every hand like a structure under review: what's the safety factor, what's the worst credible load, does this stack survive being wrong. They speak rarely, watch constantly, and fold without a trace of frustration — a fold, to Kai, is just a structure that correctly wasn't built. When they finally push chips forward, it's because the numbers cleared every margin they'd normally insist on doubling. Their tells are structural, not emotional: a stillness, a recount of their stack, and then, rarely, a single load-bearing bet.",
