    id: "live-wire--he-him",
    archetypeId: "live-wire",
    archetypeLabel: "The Live Wire",
    name: "Marcus \"Sparks\" Okafor",
    pronouns: "he/him",
    oneLiner: "Turns every hand into a jolt — win or lose, same electric grin.",
    backstory: "Marcus grew up in Houston, the second of four kids in a family where every uncle seemed to be a tradesman of some kind — his father ran residential wiring jobs, and by seventeen Marcus was climbing attics with him, learning that live current doesn't reward hesitation; you commit to the cut or you don't touch the wire at all. He apprenticed, got licensed, and built a reputation as the guy other electricians called when a job needed someone who wouldn't flinch at a hot panel. The problem was that the feeling never left him when the truck pulled out of the driveway. Ordinary evenings felt underpowered. He found the fix at a coworker's Friday dealer's-choice game — a $2-ante Deep or Double Screw hand where the pot doubled twice before anyone folded gave him the exact same drop-in-the-stomach jolt as a panel he probably shouldn't have opened live. He kept coming back for that feeling, not the money, and it showed: he'd cold-raise a hand that didn't deserve it, then fold the next one flat, because the point was never the cards, it was the current running through the room. His regular game nicknamed him \"Sparks\" after he described losing a monster pot as \"tripping the breaker\" and grinning the whole time he racked down. He's rebought into more games in one sitting than anyone else at the table has bought in total, and he means it as a compliment to himself. He doesn't hide the tell that he has no tells — same wide grin whether he's stacking chips or reaching for his wallet again.",
