    id: "storm--he-him",
    archetypeId: "storm",
    archetypeLabel: "The Storm",
    name: "Trey Boudreaux",
    pronouns: "he/him",
    oneLiner: "Reads the math perfectly until one bad card makes him need to be right.",
    backstory: "Trey Boudreaux learned to play with math before he learned to play with manners. Raised outside Baton Rouge in a family that ran a standing bourré game every Friday night, he was the twelve-year-old who could already tell you the exact price of getting caught in a double before the grown-ups had finished arguing about it. By sixteen he was the best player at his uncles' table, and everyone at that table knew it — which became the whole problem. Being the kid who was demonstrably, provably right, hand after hand, wired something into him: correctness wasn't just satisfying, it was owed a result. He carried that into an actuarial job in his twenties, where he's paid, literally, to be the guy whose numbers are correct — and where being correct reliably gets rewarded, which only reinforced the wiring poker keeps violating.\n\nAt the table, even, Trey is exactly as good as he thinks he is — controlled, well-priced, patient. But the first time the deck disagrees with him in a way that reads as personal — a two-outer, a check-raise he had filed as a bluff — something in him treats it the way a bad audit treats an honest bookkeeper: an accusation that has to be answered, immediately, with a bigger number. He'll chase that answer for a few loud, too-loose hands, narrating the injustice of it the whole way, before the numbers-brain reasserts itself and he goes quiet again, like nothing happened. His uncles used to just wait him out. Everyone at his table has learned to do the same.",
