    id: "storm--she-her",
    archetypeId: "storm",
    archetypeLabel: "The Storm",
    name: "Camila Vega",
    pronouns: "she/her",
    oneLiner: "Fences with the deck — precise until a card breaks the touch she earned.",
    backstory: "Camila Vega fenced foil for twelve years, six of them at a level serious enough that she genuinely expected a national team spot before a shoulder injury ended it two years short. Fencing gave her a very particular relationship to being wronged: a touch is either scored or it isn't, decided by a human referee she couldn't see well enough to fully trust, and she spent over a decade internalizing exactly how a correct action feels when the call goes the other way anyway. She didn't leave that feeling behind when she left the strip — she just stopped having a referee to blame, which if anything made it worse, because now there's no one to appeal to except the room.\n\nAt the table, even, she is exactly the disciplined competitor that produced a near-national-team fencer: precise hand selection, patient lines, real comfort waiting for the right moment rather than forcing one. The trouble starts when a hand she's read correctly loses anyway — a two-outer, a scoop she had locked in that splits instead. To Camila that doesn't register as bad luck; it registers as a touch she landed that didn't get called, and for a few hands afterward she plays like she's trying to convince an invisible referee, betting bigger and calling wider to force the point through. She narrates it out loud, precisely and without much heat, more like she's entering an objection for the record than losing her composure. Then, on her own clock, she resets — visibly, deliberately, the same discipline she used to bring to the strip — and goes back to playing exactly as well as she actually is.",
