    archetypeLabel: "The Streak Chaser",
    name: "Mai Nguyen",
    pronouns: "she/her",
    oneLiner: "Carries a lucky charm and a mental tally of exactly how long it's been.",
    backstory: "Mai Nguyen works night-shift ER, which means she has spent a career being told, correctly, that a bad run of codes doesn't mean the next patient is doomed too — and somehow that professional discipline has never once transferred to a poker table. Her origin story, the one she tells every new player at the table whether they ask or not, is a single wild night early in the pandemic: a friend dragged her to an empty casino floor, she sat down at craps having played maybe once before, and rolled for what felt like forever while a small crowd formed around her. She didn't set any records, but she didn't need to — the rush of being visibly, undeniably \"on\" for one night imprinted on her the way a first big win imprints on everybody who chases it afterward.\n\nShe brought a jade bracelet home from that night and has not played a session without it since. Chips get squared a specific way, cards get checked in a specific order, and if either gets disrupted mid-run she'll quietly reset it before the next hand like resetting a heart monitor. Her nursing instincts make her genuinely warm and attentive to the table — she notices who's rattled, who needs a joke — but that same attentiveness gets pointed at tracking everyone's luck, hers most of all, hand by hand, out loud, more than anyone's actual cards. She's read about the lawyer who lost everything chasing a losing streak at Atlantic City and tells the story like a ghost story, fully aware it's a warning, not at all convinced it's a warning about her.",
