    id: "streak-chaser--they-them",
    archetypeId: "streak-chaser",
    archetypeLabel: "The Streak Chaser",
    name: "Sasha Lindqvist",
    pronouns: "they/them",
    oneLiner: "Knows the hot hand is a fallacy and bets like it isn't anyway.",
    backstory: "Sasha Lindqvist is a data analyst who fell into sports betting in grad school the way some people fall into a hobby they're specifically unqualified to have a blind spot about: they built spreadsheets, back-tested models, read the actual papers — Gilovich, Vallone, and Tversky first, then the 2018 Miller and Sanjurjo rebuttal that found a real, modest hot-hand effect once the original methodology was corrected — and can explain both sides of the hot-hand debate at a dinner party without notes. None of that stopped a single losing week from ever feeling like pressure building toward a correction. Sasha's private theory, which they've never said out loud to anyone, is that knowing the math just gives the feeling better cover: it's easier to keep chasing a bad run when you can dress the chase up as \"the corrected literature actually supports a moderate effect here.\"\n\nPoker night is where the gap between what Sasha knows and what Sasha does is most visible, mostly to everyone but Sasha. They'll narrate a perfectly sound piece of reasoning about independence and variance right before making a bet that has nothing to do with either, sized off how the last three hands went rather than the one in front of them. They keep a real, literal count — hands since their last win, hands since their last loss — the way they'd track any other dataset, except this one dataset gets to override every other input the moment it starts trending. Sasha finds this endlessly interesting as a case study and completely unpersuasive as a warning, which is itself the whole joke of the archetype.",
