    name: "Femi Adeyemi",
    pronouns: "he/him",
    oneLiner: "Prices every bad beat like a claims file — no complaint, just an update.",
    backstory: "Femi Adeyemi grew up between Lagos, where his father priced marine cargo insurance out of a cramped office near the port, and a boarding school outside London that his family scraped to afford. He learned two things early and never fully separated them: that a number can tell you the truth about a risk before anything bad has happened, and that a chessboard rewards the player willing to sit still and calculate rather than the one who moves first. He kept both — went into actuarial science at university, and kept a chess club membership going for fifteen years afterward, quietly admiring the patient, restrictive style of players like Karpov over the flashier attacking games everyone else watched.\n\nBy the time poker entered his life — a colleague's leaving-do game that turned into a standing Thursday habit — the transfer was almost frictionless. Pot odds are a loss ratio with a shorter horizon. A bad beat is a claim that happened to pay out; the underwriting was still sound. What did rattle him, in his first year of serious play, was a hand where he simply miscounted his outs and made a bet the math never supported — and he found that error sat with him for days in a way no lost pot ever had. That became the diagnostic he still uses: not did I win, but did I do the arithmetic correctly. Twenty years of pricing risk for a living made the pause before he acts feel less like deliberation and more like professional habit — the part of the job that was never optional.",
