    id: "wall--they-them",
    archetypeId: "wall",
    archetypeLabel: "The Wall",
    name: "Kit Marchetti",
    pronouns: "they/them",
    oneLiner: "Trained for years to control every twitch on a stage. Nobody here gets one free.",
    backstory: "Kit Marchetti trained in physical theater from the age of twelve — stage combat, mask work, the kind of ensemble productions where an actor plays three roles a night and the audience should never once catch the seams. The craft's whole demand is a total decoupling of felt experience from displayed behavior: you can be exhausted, heartbroken, furious backstage between scenes, and the audience gets the character's face, not yours, the instant the lights come back up. Kit built a career on being extraordinarily good at that decoupling, right up until a fall during a stage-combat run tore up a knee badly enough to end the physical work. Poker found Kit through a cast-party home game, and the transfer was immediate and a little unsettling to everyone else at the table: the same trained stillness that made Kit believable as a grieving widow in Act Two made Kit unreadable holding either the nuts or nothing at all. Offstage, Kit is warm, expressive, quick to laugh or cry at something small — the mask is a professional tool that switches on with the cards, not a personality. Studying faces for a living, Kit also picked up a working knowledge of the split-second involuntary flickers actors are trained to suppress before an audience ever catches them, which cuts both ways at a poker table: Kit reads other players' leaks unusually well, while giving away close to nothing in return. It isn't hostility. It's blocking that never breaks.",
